# WEEK – 2  
# Amazon EBS Volume Management and EC2 Scaling
## Aim
1. Create an EC2 instance.
2. Scale the instance by changing the instance type.
3. Create and attach an EBS volume to the EC2 instance.
4. Create and attach the same EBS volume to another EC2 instance in the same AWS region.
5. Create a snapshot of the EBS volume and copy it to another AWS region.
6. Create a new volume from the snapshot and attach it to an EC2 instance in another region.
---
# Part A — Create an EC2 Instance

## Step 1
Open AWS Console:
https://aws.amazon.com
Login to your AWS account.
## Step 2
In the top search bar, type:
EC2
Click **EC2**.
## Step 3
In the left sidebar:
Instances → Launch Instances
## Step 4
Under **Name and tags**, enter:
Instance-A
## Step 5
Under **Application and OS Images (AMI)**:
- Select **Ubuntu**
- Leave default Ubuntu Server LTS
## Step 6
Under **Instance type**, select:
t2.nano
## Step 7
Under **Key pair (login)**:
Click:
Create new key pair
Use:
| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Key pair name | `ebslabkey` |
| Key pair type | RSA |
| Private key format | `.pem` |
Click:
Create key pair
The `.pem` file downloads automatically.
## Step 8
Under **Network settings**, ensure:
Allow SSH traffic from Anywhere (0.0.0.0/0)
is checked.
## Step 9
Click:
Launch Instance
## Step 10
Click:
View All Instances
Wait until:
Instance State: Running
Status Checks: 2/2 Passed

# Part B — Scale the EC2 Instance

## Step 11
Select the EC2 instance.
## Step 12
Click:
Instance State → Stop Instance
Wait until instance state becomes:
Stopped
## Step 13
Click:
Actions → Instance Settings → Change Instance Type
## Step 14
Select a larger instance type.
Example:
t2.micro
or
t2.small
## Step 15
Click:
Apply
## Step 16
Start the instance again:
Instance State → Start Instance
The instance now has increased CPU and RAM.

---

# Part C — Create and Attach an EBS Volume

## Step 17
In the EC2 Dashboard left sidebar:
Elastic Block Store → Volumes
## Step 18
Click:
Create Volume
## Step 19
Configure the volume.
Example settings:
| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Volume Type | gp3 |
| Size | 10 GiB |
| Availability Zone | Same as EC2 instance |

> IMPORTANT: The Availability Zone must match the EC2 instance AZ.

## Step 20
Click:
Create Volume

# Part D — Attach EBS Volume to EC2 Instance

## Step 21
Select the newly created volume.
## Step 22
Click:
Actions → Attach Volume
## Step 23
Select:

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Instance | Instance-A |
| Device Name | `/dev/sdf` |

## Step 24
Click:
Attach Volume

# Part E — Connect to EC2 Using SSH

## Step 25
Select the EC2 instance.
Copy the:
Public IPv4 Address
## Step 26
Open PowerShell.
Navigate to the folder containing the `.pem` file.
Example:
cd Downloads

## Step 27
Connect using SSH.
ssh -i "ebslabkey.pem" ubuntu@YOUR_PUBLIC_IP

Example:
ssh -i "ebslabkey.pem" ubuntu@54.90.10.20

Type:
yes
if prompted.

# Part F — Verify the Attached EBS Volume

## Step 28
Check available disks:
lsblk
## Step 29
Check filesystem details:
lsblk -fs
# Part G — Create Partition on the New Volume

## Step 30
Run:
sudo fdisk /dev/nvme1n1
## Step 31
Inside fdisk:
Press:
m
to view help menu.
## Step 32
Create partition using:
n → p → Enter → Enter → Enter
## Step 33
Save changes:
w

# Part H — Format the Partition

## Step 34
Format the new partition:
sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/nvme1n1p1

# Part I — Create Mount Directory

## Step 35
Create mount directory:
sudo mkdir /mnt/ebstry
# Part J — Mount the Volume
## Step 36
Mount the partition:
sudo mount /dev/nvme1n1p1 /mnt/ebstry
## Step 37
Verify mount:
df -h
## Step 38
Verify blocks:
lsblk
# Part K — Create Data on Volume
## Step 39
Create a sample file:
sudo touch /mnt/ebstry/testfile.txt
## Step 40
Verify:
ls /mnt/ebstry
# Part L — Detach Volume and Attach to Another Instance in Same Region

## Step 41
Go to EC2 Console.
## Step 42
Select the volume.
## Step 43
Click:
Actions → Detach Volume
# Part M — Create Another EC2 Instance

## Step 44
Launch another EC2 instance.

Name:
Instance-B
Use same:
- AMI
- Key pair
- Availability Zone

# Part N — Attach Volume to Instance-B

## Step 45
Go to:
Volumes
Select the detached volume.
## Step 46
Click:
Actions → Attach Volume
## Step 47
Instance-B
Click:
Attach Volume
# Part O — Connect to Instance-B

Copy Public IPv4 of Instance-B.
Open PowerShell and connect:
ssh -i "ebslabkey.pem" ubuntu@INSTANCE_B_IP

# Part P — Mount Existing Volume on Instance-B

Verify attached disk:
lsblk
Create mount directory:
sudo mkdir /mnt/ebstry
Mount volume:
sudo mount /dev/nvme1n1p1 /mnt/ebstry
## Step 53
Verify data:
ls /mnt/ebstry
The previous data should be available.

# Part Q — Create Snapshot of EBS Volume

## Step 54
Detach the volume from Instance-B.
Actions → Detach Volume
Select the volume.
Click:
Actions → Create Snapshot
Enter snapshot name.
ebs-snapshot
Click:
Create Snapshot

# Part R — Copy Snapshot to Another Region

Go to:
Elastic Block Store → Snapshots
Select the snapshot.
Click:
Actions → Copy Snapshot
Choose destination region:
US West (Oregon)
Click:
Copy Snapshot
Wait for completion.
# Part S — Switch to Destination Region


From top-right AWS region selector:
Switch to:
Oregon (us-west-2)

# Part T — Create Volume from Snapshot

## Step 64
Go to:
Snapshots
Select copied snapshot.
## Step 65
Click:
Actions → Create Volume from Snapshot
Select Availability Zone.
Click:
Create Volume

# Part U — Launch New EC2 Instance in Destination Region

Create a new EC2 instance in Oregon region.
Example name:
Instance-Oregon
Use Ubuntu AMI.

---

# Part V — Attach Volume to New Instance

## Step 68
Go to:
Volumes
Select the new volume.
## Step 69
Click:
Actions → Attach Volume
## Step 70
Select:
Instance-Oregon
Click:
Attach Volume

# Part W — Connect to Oregon Instance

## Step 71
Copy Public IPv4 address.
Open PowerShell:
ssh -i "ebslabkey.pem" ubuntu@OREGON_INSTANCE_IP

# Part X — Mount the Volume

Verify attached disk:
lsblk

Create mount directory:
sudo mkdir /mnt/ebstry

Mount the volume:
sudo mount /dev/nvme1n1p1 /mnt/ebstry

Verify mounted storage:
df -h

# Result
Successfully:

- Created and scaled an EC2 instance
- Created and attached an EBS volume
- Mounted the volume in Linux
- Shared the volume between EC2 instances
- Created and copied snapshots across regions
- Restored and mounted the volume in another AWS region
